<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Maine</h1><p class="st-meta">1622 CE – 1658 CE · capital York</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A proprietary colony granted to Sir Ferdinando Gorges in what is now southern Maine, absorbed by Massachusetts in the 1650s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1622 the Council for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-gorges">Sir Ferdinando Gorges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-mason-governor">John Mason</a> the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merrimack-river">Merrimack</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kennebec-river">Kennebec</a> rivers. When they divided it in 1629, Gorges took the eastern part. Fishing stations and small settlements grew along the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kittery-maine">Kittery</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saco-maine">Saco</a>, and in 1639 a royal charter made Gorges proprietor of the Province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-gorges">Gorges</a> never crossed the Atlantic, ruling through deputies such as his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-gorges">Thomas Gorges</a>. He planned an Anglican, manorial society and in 1641 chartered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> as Gorgeana, the first English city in America. In practice settlers lived from fishing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and fur trading and quarrelled with the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lygonia">Lygonia</a> grant to the north. After Gorges died in 1647, the towns organised their own government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay Colony</a> claimed that its charter covered the region and absorbed the towns between 1652 and 1658, organising them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> County. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Massachusetts</a> bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-gorges">Gorges</a> heirs' claim in 1677, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a> remained part of Massachusetts until statehood in 1820.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-maine.jpg" alt="Province of Maine" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: Martocticvs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:English_Red_Ensign_1620.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1622 CE – 1658 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>York</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinando Gorges, Thomas Gorges</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Puritanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maine (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=province-of-maine" aria-label="Province of Maine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4656 326 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Maine lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4976 582L4970 663M4976 582L3283 922"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0M3283 922h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 582h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4976 582h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks York. <a href="/atlas?year=1640&amp;focus=province-of-maine">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
